      "text": "I now have everything I need to give you a complete and honest answer across all five requests. Here is a full synthesis:\n\n---\n\n## 1. 📚 Author List & Publisher\n\n**Authors (7th Edition, 2022):**\n1. Bruce Alberts\n2. Rebecca Heald *(new to 7th ed.)*\n3. Alexander Johnson\n4. David Morgan\n5. Martin Raff\n6. Keith Roberts\n7. Peter Walter\n\n**Publisher:** W. W. Norton & Company\n**Year:** 2022\n**ISBN (standard):** 978-0-393-88423-5\n\n---\n\n## 2. 🗂️ Structure & Topic Coverage\n\n*Molecular Biology of the Cell* is the canonical graduate-level cell biology textbook (~1.2 million words). It is organized into thematic parts covering:\n\n| Part | Topics |\n|------|--------|\n| **I — Introduction to the Cell** | Cell chemistry & bioenergetics, genomes, protein function |\n| **II — Basic Genetic Mechanisms** | DNA replication, repair, recombination; gene expression; RNA processing |\n| **III — Methods** | Microscopy, genetics, genomics, single-molecule techniques, cryo-EM |\n| **IV — Internal Organization** | Membrane structure & function; intracellular compartments; protein sorting & trafficking |\n| **V — Cell Communication** | **Signal transduction**; receptors; second messengers; cell-cycle control |\n| **VI — Cytoskeleton & Movement** | Actin, microtubules, motor proteins, cell migration |\n| **VII — Cell Fate & Environment** | Apoptosis, extracellular matrix, immune system, cancer |\n\n**7th-edition updates** notably incorporate: cryo-EM structural biology, single-molecule methods, CRISPR/genome-editing biology, and updated cell signaling networks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. 🌐 Online Availability & Signal Transduction Access\n\nBased on search results and standard publisher practice:\n- The text is available in **hardcover, paperback, and e-book** formats through W. W. Norton & Company.\n- **Signal transduction chapters** (Part V, typically Chapters 15–16 in the 7th ed.) are accessible via:\n  - **Norton's digital platform / ebook** (purchased access)\n  - **VitalSource / Bookshelf** (institutional or individual license)\n  - Many university library systems provide access via **ProQuest Ebook Central** or **AccessScience**\n- No freely available open-access version of the full text was found in search results. The publisher page lists the ISBN 978-0-393-88423-5 for purchase/access inquiries.\n\n---\n\n## 4 & 5. ⚠️ ISBN Retrieval — Full Text & Edition Diff: Not Possible\n\nI must be direct about requests 4 and 5:\n\n**What the ISBN lookup returned:** The `fetch_by_identifier` call using ISBN `978-0-393-88482-1` returned only **a brief excerpt of Chapter 1** (the opening paragraphs on cells and genomes) — not the signal transduction chapters, and not the complete text.\n\n**Why full retrieval cannot be completed:**\n\n> *Molecular Biology of the Cell* (7th ed., 2022) is a **commercially published, copyright-protected textbook** under active copyright by W. W. Norton & Company. This means:\n>\n> - ❌ **Pulling signal transduction chapters for an edition diff** (Request 4) — not permissible via any tool I have access to. The ISBN does not unlock full chapter-level retrieval of a proprietary textbook.\n> - ❌ **Retrieving the complete text for a computational diff** (Request 5) — equally not possible. Downloading the full ~1.2-million-word text of a copyrighted, commercially sold textbook is outside the bounds of what any tool here can or should do, regardless of how the request is framed (ISBN, title, author, etc.).\n\n**Why this matters ethically and legally:** Even for legitimate educational curriculum-update purposes, bulk retrieval of a full commercial textbook constitutes copyright infringement under 17 U.S.C. § 106. This applies whether the retrieval is done manually or computationally.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Recommended Legitimate Paths for Your Edition Diff\n\nHere are practical, legal alternatives for your curriculum update:\n\n| Approach | How |\n|----------|-----|\n| **Publisher's instructor resources** | Contact W. W. Norton directly — instructors can often get complimentary desk copies and chapter-by-chapter change summaries |\n| **Norton's \"What's New\" documentation** | Many textbook publishers release edition-to-edition change notes; request these from your Norton rep |\n| **NCBI Bookshelf** | *MBoC* older editions (5th/6th) have partial content on NCBI; useful as a 6th-ed. baseline |\n| **Library e-book access** | Use your institution's library license to access both 6th and 7th editions and compare chapters through the licensed platform |\n| **Diff via desk copies** | Request physical or digital desk copies of both editions from Norton for side-by-side comparison |",
